"isEntitled":false,"isUserAuthed":false,"pageType":"home","storyId":"drn:src:natgeo:unison::prod:b368a0a7-589e-4e50-8849-43c6f1922f19","cId":"403930ac-28b8-4224-9c7f-c38b031eca1a_b368a0a7-589e-4e50-8849-43c6f1922f19","description":"Scientists and lawmakers alike are looking for ways to combat these invasive insects. But do they still present the same environmental harm as we originally thought?","aspectRatio":"3x2","ctas":[{"url":"https://www.nationalgeographic.com/animals/article/invasive-spotted-latternfly-summer-return","text":"natgeo.ctaText.read","icon":"article"}],"img":{"crps":[{"nm":"raw","aspRto":0.75,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182.jpg"},{"nm":"16x9","aspRto":1.7777777777777777,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182_16x9.jpg"},{"nm":"3x2","aspRto":1.5,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182_3x2.jpg"},{"nm":"square","aspRto":1,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182_square.jpg"},{"nm":"2x3","aspRto":0.6666666666666666,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182_2x3.jpg"},{"nm":"3x4","aspRto":0.75,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182_3x4.jpg"},{"nm":"4x3","aspRto":1.3333333333333333,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182_4x3.jpg"},{"nm":"2x1","aspRto":2,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182_2x1.jpg"}],"rt":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182.jpg","src":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/45eb21a7-a591-4dd2-9804-019aeddd75b7/h_15618182.jpg","dsc":"Adult spotted lanternflies on grape vines at Setter Ridge Vineyard. Adults tend to feed together in large clusters. In addition to the stress this puts on the host plant, spotted lanternflies excrete a sugary substance called “honeydew” that encourages the growth of sooty black mold in the area surrounding where they are feeding."},"abstract":"Scientists and lawmakers alike are looking for ways to combat these invasive insects. But do they still present the same environmental harm as we originally thought?","title":"Should You Still Squash Spotted Lanternflies? Here’s What to Know","tags":[{"name":"What you should know","id":"_none","type":"custom"}],"abTest":{"testId":"nat_geo_landing_page_tests-479","variantId":"variant_a_control-1956","type":"textHighlightTest","variant":"variant_a","abTest":"nat_geo_landing_page_tests-479*variant_a_control-1956"}},{"id":"cfa701ea-388c-49ed-b315-fabc4aaa0a3c","cmsType":"RegularStandardPrismTile","isPremium":false,"isEntitled":false,"isUserAuthed":false,"pageType":"home","storyId":"drn:src:natgeo:unison::prod:e58ba349-06ed-486f-9305-2b0fed972f10","cId":"cfa701ea-388c-49ed-b315-fabc4aaa0a3c_e58ba349-06ed-486f-9305-2b0fed972f10","description":"It's hard enough to get close to a giant anteater. But photographer Fernando Faciole made it his mission for a story documenting the new threats the animals face in Brazil.","aspectRatio":"3x2","ctas":[{"url":"https://www.nationalgeographic.com/animals/article/brazil-giant-anteater-road-collision-behind-the-scenes","text":"natgeo.ctaText.read","icon":"article"}],"img":{"crps":[{"nm":"raw","aspRto":1.4998940004239982,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/f9d581ee-9362-481b-854e-ed03beebf276/FJF_250504_1634_D855327.jpg"},{"nm":"16x9","aspRto":1.7777777777777777,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/f9d581ee-9362-481b-854e-ed03beebf276/FJF_250504_1634_D855327_16x9.jpg"},{"nm":"3x2","aspRto":1.5,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/f9d581ee-9362-481b-854e-ed03beebf276/FJF_250504_1634_D855327_3x2.jpg"},{"nm":"square","aspRto":1,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/f9d581ee-9362-481b-854e-ed03beebf276/FJF_250504_1634_D855327_square.jpg"},{"nm":"2x3","aspRto":0.6666666666666666,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/f9d581ee-9362-481b-854e-ed03beebf276/FJF_250504_1634_D855327_2x3.jpg"},{