ural attractions was born.","aspectRatio":"3x2","ctas":[{"url":"https://www.nationalgeographic.com/environment/article/inside-the-largest-geode","text":"natgeo.ctaText.read","icon":"article"}],"img":{"crps":[{"nm":"raw","aspRto":0.9072265625,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234.jpg"},{"nm":"16x9","aspRto":1.7777777777777777,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234_16x9.jpg"},{"nm":"3x2","aspRto":1.5,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234_3x2.jpg"},{"nm":"square","aspRto":1,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234_square.jpg"},{"nm":"2x3","aspRto":0.6666666666666666,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234_2x3.jpg"},{"nm":"3x4","aspRto":0.75,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234_3x4.jpg"},{"nm":"4x3","aspRto":1.3333333333333333,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234_4x3.jpg"},{"nm":"2x1","aspRto":2,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234_2x1.jpg"}],"rt":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234.jpg","src":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/37ff3875-03b5-4e16-8f7b-3f8690695be4/MM10453_H-260216-00234.jpg","dsc":"Sparkliest Cave, Put-in-Bay, Ohio","ttl":"Sparkliest cave"},"abstract":"Millions of years of percolating water and geological dumb luck created Crystal Cave, an uncannily pristine chamber beneath a Lake Erie island. When a winemaker accidentally drilled into it, one of the United States’ most surprising natural attractions was born.","title":"Inside the Geode as Big as a Garage","tags":[{"name":"Wonders of America","id":"_none","type":"custom"}],"abTest":{"testId":"nat_geo_landing_page_tests-479","variantId":"varia